      "description": "Ink block, square; the one decisive action.",
      "html": "<button class=\"ds-btn\">Clear all filters</button>",
      "css": ".ds-btn{height:40px;padding:0 24px;border:0;border-radius:0;background:var(--primary);color:var(--primary-foreground);font:500 .875rem Barlow,sans-serif;cursor:pointer;transition:background-color .15s}.ds-btn:hover{background:color-mix(in oklab,var(--primary) 85%,transparent)}.ds-btn:focus-visible{outline:2px solid var(--ring);outline-offset:2px}"
    },
    {
      "name": "Outline Button",
      "kind": "button",
      "refersTo": "button-outline",
      "description": "2px ink rule on label stock; Show more, Undo, Clear list.",
      "html": "<button class=\"ds-btn-o\">Show more</button>",
      "css": ".ds-btn-o{height:40px;padding:0 24px;border:2px solid var(--border);border-radius:0;background:var(--background);color:var(--foreground);font:500 .875rem Barlow,sans-serif;cursor:pointer;transition:background-color .15s}.ds-btn-o:hover{background:var(--muted)}.ds-btn-o:focus-visible{outline:2px solid var(--ring);outline-offset:2px}"
    }
  ],
  "narrative": {
    "northStar": "The Consignment Label",
    "overview": "Every deal is a parcel on a kraft carton. The page ground is the carton itself, warm brown and matte; each product or bundle is a thermal-white label stuck to it, ruled in 2px ink with square corners and no shadow. Today's price is printed reversed in a black routing block, the winning bundle is counted out as a consignment manifest (\"PCS 1/2\"), and status that needs attention travels on a hi-vis yellow handling band under a black top bar. The world reads like road freight paperwork: dense, legible at arm's length, nothing decorative that is not also information.\n\nDensity is high and deliberate. Labels sit in a 4-up grid on desktop, the filter rail is a stack of ruled chips with their counts printed on them, and rank inside a label is made by weight, case and reversal (routing block, condensed caps, bold) rather than a third type size. Colour is rationed: green means a saving, red means dearer than the parts or above the usual price, yellow means take note. Everything else is ink, label stock and carton.\n\nAt night the same carton goes charcoal-brown, labels turn dark, and the routing block flips to a light block on dark stock. The black bar and the hi-vis band do not change: they are the fixed livery of the site.",
    "keyCharacteristics": [
      "Kraft-carton ground, thermal-white label stock, 2px ink rules, square corners, flat.",
      "Price printed reversed in a routing block; the winning bundle shown as a manifest.",
      "Barlow for reading, Barlow Condensed caps for labels, prices and headings; tabular figures everywhere.",
      "Three signal colours with fixed meanings, and nothing else coloured.",
      "Black top bar and hi-vis handling band, identical in light and dark."
    ],
    "rules": [
      {
        "name": "The Three Signals Rule",
        "body": "Green is a saving, red is dearer than the parts or above usual, yellow is \"take note\". No colour carries any other meaning, and no element is coloured for decoration.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Fixed Livery Rule",
        "body": "The black bar and the hi-vis band keep the same colours in light and dark mode; only the carton and the labels change.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Ink Border Rule",
        "body": "A label's outer edge is 2px of the ink token (in dark mode the lighter `border-night`). Inner divisions use the 1px hairline rule. Never invert that.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Readout Rule",
        "body": "Tabular figures everywhere; never switch a number to proportional figures to make it look tighter.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Three Levers Rule",
        "body": "Inside a label, rank comes from weight, case and reversal, not from adding another size. If a new element needs emphasis, make it bold, condensed caps or reversed before making it bigger.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Flat Stock Rule",
        "body": "Labels, chips, buttons, badges and the manifest have no shadow at rest or on hover; state changes are fill changes.",
        "section": "elevation"
      }
    ],
    "dos": [
      "Do build every new surface out of label stock on carton: label fill, 2px ink border, square corners, no shadow.",
      "Do print the deciding number in a routing block (ink fill, condensed bold, tabular figures) with its comparison beside it.",
      "Do put the caveat next to the number it qualifies, as a \"how\" line, a badge title or a band entry.",
      "Do show a filter's result count on the chip, and when it is 0, disable it with a dashed border and say why.",
      "Do keep green for savings, red for dearer or above usual, and hi-vis yellow for take-note, in both themes.",
      "Do grow touch targets to 44px on coarse pointers."
    ],
    "donts": [
      "Don't use rounded corners, soft cards or drop shadows on labels, chips, buttons or badges; the only shadow is the floating autocomplete list.",
      "Don't colour anything for decoration, including a blue or brand accent; there are three signal colours and they mean one thing each.",
      "Don't use red for errors, destructive actions or emphasis; red means dearer than the parts or above the usual price.",
      "Don't add a third size inside a label to rank things; use weight, condensed caps or reversal.",
      "Don't draw a sparkline for a price that has not moved.",
      "Don't change the black bar or the hi-vis band between light and dark mode.",
      "Don't borrow 4WD Supacentre or Adventure Kings logos, colours or branding; the site's own mark is the hi-vis tag."
    ]
  }
}